OpenRSP bridge firmware, set "own" — the images the Mac app and the Android apps ship
=====================================================================================

What:     fw_common.bin, fw_rsp1.bin, fw_rspduo.bin + manifest.sha256 — the 8051 USB bridge firmware the
          RSP driver uploads to the receiver at every power-up (it is never stored on the receiver).
Source:   vendor/openrsp/firmware/src (GPL-3.0-or-later, the OpenRSP authors; see vendor/openrsp/COPYING).
          These are BUILT FROM THAT SOURCE: `make -C vendor/openrsp/firmware/src own` with sdcc 4.2.0
          (Debian bookworm) reproduces them byte for byte.
Release:  identical to the images in OpenRSP's own 3.15.2-20 package (openrsp_3.15.2-20_arm64.deb, SHA-256
          ed6961277b1019125bd517ec70e8cd17b75a26e198fc3a464b8901a98f26e58a, per openrsp.dev SHA256SUMS),
          /usr/share/sdrplay_api/firmware/own — the images upstream tested on real receivers.

★★★ WHY A PINNED COPY AND NOT A BUILD STEP (2026-10-22). The Linux packages build these from source with
    the build image's sdcc (vibeserver/CMakeLists.txt, "OpenRSP"). The Mac and the Android Gradle build have
    no sdcc 4.2.0: Homebrew's sdcc 4.6.0 builds the same source into DIFFERENT images (fw_common.bin
    0171e881…, not 0508cc60…). A different compiler's firmware is firmware nobody has run on a receiver, and
    this is the one component that talks to the RSP's USB bridge directly — so those builds ship the tested
    images instead, and check them against this manifest at build time AND (Android) at run time.
  ★ To re-verify: in any bookworm container, `apt install sdcc make python3`, `make -C firmware/src own
    O=/tmp/fw` from vendor/openrsp, then `diff /tmp/fw/own_build/manifest.sha256 own/manifest.sha256`.
  ★ On an OpenRSP update: replace vendor/openrsp, rebuild the firmware on Linux, compare its manifest with
    the new upstream package's, then copy the images here in the same commit.
